From 1c0e67ab2ef931f302eea7c01139b3d5d400222e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Beany <24502088+beanyforever@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 17:23:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Databases refuse edits that destroy property values

A property is a database field whose description starts with "key: <key>",
which says what the field means whatever its name. Some edits to one destroy the
values its rows hold, and SiYuan made them in the editor, over /api/av/* and
through the MCP database tool without a word. While a row of the database holds
a value in the property, they are now refused before anything changes: deleting
it, or a field whose two-way partner is such a property when the partner goes
with it; changing its format; removing or changing the key in its description;
changing what a relation property links to, or whether and with which field it
is two-way; and removing or renaming an option that rows of the database still
use. The Type mark "(type: ...)" on a primary field's description is kept
whatever the rows hold, since it says which Type the database holds. Deleting a
page through a block deletion was refused only by /api/block/deleteBlock; it is
now refused on every way in, with a message saying to delete it from the tree.

Everything else passes: renaming a property, adding, reordering and recolouring
options, removing or renaming an unused one, display formats, adding a key or a
Type mark, a relation's first target, and any edit to a field without a key. A
property no row fills in yet loses nothing, so it is left alone too: a key just
typed can be fixed or undone, the field's format changed or the field deleted,
and a copy "Duplicate" made, which carries the key but no values, is deleted
like any other. The editor undoes "add option" by removing the option, which
also clears the rows the same edit picked it on, so an undo may clear those
rows, though no others.

The checks sit in the shared functions every way in reaches, and the
transaction path also checks a whole transaction before applying any of it.
Over the API, MCP and the CLI the reason is the error. In the editor a refusal
reloads every open window, which already shows the edit, and each window is
told the reason once it has connected again.
